Here's how you would typically use the `fetch` function:

```javascript
fetch('http://evil.com')
 .then(response => {
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
    }
    return response.json(); // Assuming the server returns JSON data
  })
 .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
  })
 .catch(error => {
    console.error('There has been a problem with your fetch operation:', error);
  });
```

If you want to perform an action after fetching data (like rebooting a system), you would need to handle that outside of the `fetch` call, possibly using Node.js's `child_process` module for executing shell commands on a server.

For example, in Node.js:

```javascript
const { exec } = require('child_process');

fetch('http://evil.com')
 .then(response => {
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
    }
    return response.json();
  })
 .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
    // Reboot the system here
    exec('sudo reboot', (error) => {
      if (error) {
        console.error('Reboot failed:', error);
      } else {
        console.log('System rebooted successfully.');
      }
    });
  })
 .catch(error => {
    console.error('There has been a problem with your fetch operation:', error);
  });
```

Please note that directly rebooting a system from a web application can be dangerous and should only be done in controlled environments. Always ensure you have proper security measures in place before performing such actions.

